## I. The brain's systems

### dopamine (the reward system)
Dopamine neurons of the ventral tegmental area project to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex. In Schultz's recordings (1997) their firing tracks reward prediction error, the difference between what was expected and what arrived. Addictive drugs raise dopamine directly or indirectly, so they teach the learning system a prediction error no natural reward produces at that rate.
**links:** cocaine (blocks the dopamine transporter) · methamphetamine and amphetamine (reverse the transporter and release dopamine) · opioids (disinhibit VTA dopamine neurons through GABA interneurons) · contingency management (competes with drug reward using the same learning signal) · keltner (wellbeing is not the same as reward).

### the μ-opioid system
The body's own opioids are endorphins and enkephalins, and the μ-opioid receptor carries pain relief, reward and the drive to breathe. Overdose kills by respiratory depression. Naloxone displaces opioids from the receptor and restores breathing.
**links:** oxycodone · fentanyl · methadone and buprenorphine (occupy the receptor steadily and stop the cycle of withdrawal and craving) · purdue-pharma.

### the serotonin 5-HT2A system
The classic psychedelics (psilocybin, LSD, DMT) are agonists at the 5-HT2A receptor. Under them, activity of the default mode network falls; that network carries self-referential thought.
**links:** psilocybin · lsd · dmt · keltner (awe also lowers default-mode activity and self-focus).

### the endocannabinoid system
The CB1 and CB2 receptors and their ligands. The first ligand was named **anandamide** (Devane, Mechoulam et al., 1992), from Sanskrit *ānanda*, "bliss". It is a retrograde messenger that tunes the release of other transmitters, and it takes part in the "runner's high".
**links:** cannabis (THC is a partial agonist at CB1) · keltner · docs/etymologies.md (endocannabinoid).

