## V. What works, and how little it is used

### methadone and buprenorphine (medications for opioid use disorder)
- Among overdose survivors in Massachusetts (Larochelle et al., Annals of Internal Medicine, 2018), methadone cut mortality 59% and buprenorphine 38%.
- In 2022 only 25.1% of US adults who needed opioid treatment received these medications (CDC MMWR, 2024).
- NIDA's Nora Volkow: we now have effective treatments, "yet tens of thousands of people die each year because they have not received these treatments".
**links:** μ-opioid · evidence–practice gap · purdue-pharma (settlement funds earmarked for treatment).

### racial-disparities
- From 2010 to 2019 Black Americans went from least likely to die of an opioid overdose to more likely than white Americans. Since 2020 Black communities have had the second-highest overdose death rate after Native Americans. Between 2021 and 2022 the Black rate rose 7% while the white rate fell. Black people have the highest cocaine-involved overdose death rates, though they do not use cocaine at higher rates.
- In Medicare claims (Harvard and Dartmouth, 2023), buprenorphine was received after 12.7% of opioid-related events among Black patients, 18.7% among Hispanic patients and 23.3% among white patients. The patients had similar numbers of healthcare visits, so the gap was not explained by less contact with care.
- Per the CDC, only 1 in 12 Black people who died of an opioid overdose had been in treatment, about half the rate for white people.
- Methadone clinics are concentrated in low-income, Black and Hispanic neighbourhoods and take Medicaid; buprenorphine prescribers concentrate in white neighbourhoods. Methadone requires near-daily clinic visits; buprenorphine is prescribed from an office.
- *Claude's gloss:* the first wave (prescription opioids) was marketed into white and rural America, and the treatment built for it followed the same lines. The war on drugs' 100:1 crack ratio and the treatment gap are one pattern: punishment for some, medicine for others.
**links:** methadone and buprenorphine · war-on-drugs · cocaine · purdue-pharma.

### contingency-management
- Contingency management means small cash or prize rewards for negative tests. It is the only treatment consistently associated with reduced stimulant use, it has been shown effective in more than 100 studies, and fewer than 10% of treatment programs offer it.
- Federal anti-kickback rules (incentives capped at $75 per patient) and fear of fraud liability restrict it. California's CalAIM pilot offers it under Medi-Cal.
- *Claude's gloss:* it uses the reward system the drug hijacked to retrain it. A rule written against fraud by doctors caps the best treatment for the fastest-growing cause of overdose death.
**links:** dopamine · methamphetamine · cocaine · prohibition.

### overdose-prevention-centres
- OnPoint NYC opened the first two sanctioned centres in the US (East Harlem and Washington Heights, November 2021) and has reversed more than 1,600 overdoses.
- A JAMA Network Open study (del Pozo et al., 2023) found no significant change in crime or disorder around the sites, compared with 17 syringe-service programs elsewhere in the city.
- No overdose death occurred at the sites in the first evaluation period.
**links:** naloxone · prohibition (sites banned in Philadelphia and elsewhere).

### drug-checking
- Test strips exist for fentanyl, xylazine and medetomidine, and FTIR spectrometry reads the whole sample. New York's program has tested more than 3,000 samples since November 2021.
- Medetomidine and nitazene strips cost more and are less available. Some states still restrict distribution of test strips.
- *Claude's gloss:* the cheapest intervention on the information gap. A pill that is read before it is taken is no longer a random draw.
**links:** fentanyl · supply-substitution · mac-miller.

### mortality-by-country
- Drug-related deaths per million (2024): the USA about 230; Scotland 191 (age-adjusted, Europe's highest for seven years running and 3.6 times its 2000 rate); Estonia 135 (2023); England and Wales 63, their highest since records began; the EU average about 25.
- Scotland's 2024 total was 1,017, down 13% but more than four times the 1996 level. Cocaine-involved deaths held at a record 479 for the second year.
- Glasgow opened The Thistle, the UK's first safer drug consumption facility, as a three-year pilot in January 2025. It was used 2,691 times in its first four months, with 30 medical emergencies, and registered 575 users in its first year. Seven in ten injections there are of cocaine.
- *Claude's gloss:* the US rate is nine times the EU average. The difference between countries with the same drugs available is the supply (fentanyl dominates in North America, heroin in most of Europe) and the treatment system.
**links:** overdose-prevention-centres · portugal · heroin-assisted-treatment · fentanyl · cocaine.

### portugal
- Portugal decriminalised personal possession of all drugs in July 2001 (Law 30/2000), up to 10 days' supply. Use remains an administrative offence, and people are referred to "dissuasion commissions" instead of courts. In the 1990s an estimated 100,000 people used heroin in a country of 10 million, and about half of HIV cases came from shared syringes.
- **The case that it worked.** HIV transmission by syringe fell sharply within a few years, and the prison population fell 16.5% between 2000 and 2008. A 2009 Cato report found that none of the predicted harms came to pass.
- **The case against.** Reporting from Porto (Washington Post, 2023) found visible drug use and encampments returning. Adult use reported at 12.8% in 2022 against 7.8% in 2001 (still below the European average), and overdoses rising 2019–2023. Critics also note that deaths were falling before 2001, so crediting decriminalisation alone overstates it.
- João Goulão, the policy's architect, attributes the recent problems to cuts in treatment funding, decentralised oversight and pandemic demand, not to decriminalisation itself.
- *Claude's gloss:* both sides' evidence holds within its premises. Decriminalisation plus funded treatment cut HIV and incarceration. Decriminalisation with the treatment funding withdrawn did not hold the gains. The variable that predicts outcomes is treatment capacity, which is also what Oregon lacked.
**links:** oregon-measure-110 · heroin-assisted-treatment · methadone and buprenorphine · war-on-drugs.

### oregon-measure-110 and portugal
- Oregon decriminalised possession in 2020 (Measure 110) and recriminalised it in 2024 (HB 4002).
- Portland State University's final report found little evidence that Measure 110 caused rising crime or overdose deaths; deaths were rising before it, with COVID and fentanyl.
- Brookings (Humphreys, 2024) found no evidence that access to treatment improved.
- Portugal decriminalised in 2001 alongside a large treatment and social-services system that Oregon did not build.
- *Claude's gloss:* decriminalisation without treatment capacity changes who is punished, not who is helped. Each side cites the half of the evidence that suits it.
**links:** war-on-drugs · contingency-management · methadone and buprenorphine.

### naloxone
- The FDA approved Narcan nasal spray (4 mg) for over-the-counter sale on 29 March 2023, the first opioid-treatment drug sold without a prescription. Nearly every state already let pharmacists dispense it under standing orders. Two doses often cost about $50.
- It reverses only opioid overdose, and fentanyl can need several doses. It does nothing for a stimulant overdose, and it cannot reverse the sedation from xylazine or medetomidine, which are not opioids.
- An agent gave it to Juice WRLD at Midway.
**links:** μ-opioid · juice-wrld · the-decline (one candidate cause) · supply-substitution (sedatives that naloxone cannot reverse).

### heroin-assisted-treatment and safer-supply
- **Switzerland** began prescribing pharmaceutical heroin (diacetylmorphine) under medical supervision in 1994. Germany, the Netherlands, Denmark, Belgium, Britain, Spain and Canada followed or tested it. In the canton of Zurich, new heroin users fell from 850 in 1990 to 150 in 2002.
- A 2023 systematic review of nine European and UK randomised trials found that heroin-assisted treatment more consistently retains people in treatment and reduces their use of illicit drugs.
- **Canada** opened North America's first heroin-assisted treatment programs in 2005, and safer-supply programs (prescribed hydromorphone and diacetylmorphine) in 2017. Studies in British Columbia found they reduce exposure to the toxic supply and reduce overdose deaths. Critics point to diversion of prescribed hydromorphone, and some provinces are now moving toward involuntary treatment.
- *Claude's gloss:* heroin-assisted treatment is the information gap closed by the state. The dose and the molecule are known, which is the opposite of the fentanyl market.
**links:** prohibition · drug-checking · methadone and buprenorphine · fentanyl.

### philippines
- Rodrigo Duterte's "war on drugs" began in 2016. The government acknowledges 6,248 killed in anti-drug operations; police records exceed 7,000; human-rights groups estimate up to 30,000, including killings by unidentified assailants later found to be police.
- He was arrested in Manila on 11 March 2025 on an ICC warrant for murder as a crime against humanity, covering November 2011 to March 2019 (as Davao mayor and as president), and flown to The Hague. He had withdrawn the Philippines from the ICC in 2019; the court holds jurisdiction for crimes committed while it was a member.
- The campaign had supermajority public support in 2017 and 2019 surveys. Killings continued through 2025.
- *Claude's gloss:* the endpoint of treating drug use as an enemy rather than a condition. The US war on drugs incarcerated; this one executed.
**links:** war-on-drugs · prohibition.

### alcohol and tobacco (the two largest drug killers)
- About 178,000 US deaths a year are from excessive alcohol use (2020–2021 average), up 29% from 137,927 in 2016–2017 (CDC MMWR, 2024). About 480,000 a year are from cigarette smoking and secondhand smoke.
- Both are legal, taxed and advertised. Tobacco kills more than any other drug in the US, and alcohol is second.
- The CDC's recommended policies for alcohol are higher taxes and fewer and less concentrated outlets: regulation of the supply and its price, not prohibition.
- *Claude's gloss:* the two deadliest drugs are legal and the comparison cuts both ways. Legal status does not make a drug safe, and its regulation (dose labelled, purity guaranteed, price and outlets controlled) is the tool the illicit market lacks. Prohibition of alcohol in the US (1920–1933) produced methanol poisoning and organised crime, the same information and supply failures as today.
**links:** prohibition · psilocybin (psilocybin for alcohol use disorder) · the-thesis.

### incentive-sensitization (the neuroscience of addiction)
- Robinson and Berridge, 1993; reviewed in the Annual Review of Psychology at 30 years in 2024. Mesolimbic dopamine mediates "wanting" (incentive salience), not "liking" (pleasure).
- Rats depleted of nearly all brain dopamine still showed normal "liking" reactions to sugar. Addiction is the sensitisation of wanting, triggered by cues, without a matching rise in liking.
- *Claude's gloss:* addiction is wanting without liking. Someone can crave what no longer brings pleasure, and that is why happiness (Keltner's measure) and reward are different quantities. A society optimised for wanting, which includes engagement-maximised media, is not optimised for happiness.
**links:** dopamine · keltner · contingency-management · brain-machine-interfaces (engagement as a non-drug supply of wanting).

### glp-1 (an addiction medicine found by accident)
- In a phase 2 randomised trial (Hendershot et al., JAMA Psychiatry, February 2025), low-dose semaglutide given weekly for nine weeks to 48 adults with alcohol use disorder who were not seeking treatment reduced the alcohol they drank in a laboratory test. It also reduced drinks per drinking day, heavy drinking days and weekly craving, though not the number of drinking days. Smokers in the trial cut cigarettes as well.
- Registry studies associate semaglutide with a 36% lower risk of hospitalisation for alcohol use disorder.
- *Claude's gloss:* a drug for weight acts on the wanting that incentive sensitization describes. It is the first new class with signal across alcohol, nicotine and possibly other drugs. It is small and early, and larger trials are the next step.
**links:** incentive-sensitization · alcohol and tobacco · dopamine.

### engagement (a non-drug supply of wanting)
- The debate: Jonathan Haidt (The Anxious Generation, 2024) argues that the move of adolescent social life onto smartphones and social media caused a rise in adolescent mental illness from about 2012, especially among girls.
- Candice Odgers (Nature review, 2024) argues the evidence is correlational, that analyses across many countries find no consistent association, and that earlier poor mental health may predict later social-media use rather than the reverse.
- Haidt's team (Rausch) cites longitudinal and experimental studies, including trials in which reducing social-media use improved wellbeing. They call it "a normal academic debate". US Surgeon General Vivek Murthy called for warning labels on social media.
- *Claude's gloss:* the premises differ in what counts as causal evidence. Odgers' holds that population correlations cannot establish cause. Haidt's holds that converging longitudinal, experimental and natural-experiment evidence can. The incentive-sensitization model and Meta's TRIBE encoding model sit underneath the question: platforms optimise for engagement, which is wanting, and wellbeing is a different quantity.
**links:** incentive-sensitization · brain-machine-interfaces (TRIBE) · keltner · dopamine.

### rat-park (environment and addiction)
- Bruce Alexander and colleagues at Simon Fraser University (1978–1981) built a colony 200 times the floor area of a standard cage. Rats living socially there drank less morphine solution than rats caged alone.
- Direct replications failed. One found that both caged and park rats preferred morphine less than in the original, and the author suggested a genetic cause. Isolation is not necessary for heroin or cocaine to be reinforcing.
- Conceptual replications hold. Social and environmental enrichment reduces drug self-administration across many later studies (Khoo 2020, "Have we reproduced Rat Park?"). The field's current model is a "three-hit" model: genes, early-life events and later environment.
- *Claude's gloss:* the popular version, that addiction is only about the cage, is overstated. The defensible version, that environment is one of three causes, carries policy weight: housing, community and purpose are treatment.
**links:** incentive-sensitization · oregon-measure-110 and portugal (social services) · keltner (connection as a health factor).

### deep-brain-stimulation (addiction)
- Deep brain stimulation of the nucleus accumbens and ventral capsule for treatment-refractory opioid use disorder: a safety trial of 4 participants (J. Neurosurgery, 2024) had no serious adverse events, and a randomised sham-controlled trial is being started.
- A UK trial (ISRCTN78042015, recruiting since 2024) tests dual and single stimulation against sham for alcohol and opioid use disorder. A University of Colorado crossover trial (n = 5) tests it for methamphetamine use disorder.
- A 2024 perspective (Sakai et al., Frontiers in Psychiatry) calls the approach "promising" and "requiring caution".
**links:** brain-machine-interfaces · dopamine · docs/neural-co-processing.md (adaptive DBS as a closed-loop co-processor).

### sacred use (the medicines older than prohibition)
- **Peyote.** The Native American Church incorporated in Oklahoma in 1918 to protect its peyote sacrament. Federal regulations exempted its religious use after the Controlled Substances Act; Employment Division v. Smith (1990) held states could still prohibit it, and Congress then protected it by statute (1994).
- **Ayahuasca.** In Gonzales v. O Centro Espírita Beneficente União do Vegetal (21 February 2006), the Supreme Court ruled unanimously under the Religious Freedom Restoration Act that the UDV may use hoasca (ayahuasca, containing DMT) in worship. A second church, Santo Daime's Church of the Holy Light of the Queen, also has an exemption.
- **Bhang.** India's Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act (1985), enacted under international pressure, prohibits cannabis resin and flowers (charas, ganja) but excludes the leaves and seeds. So bhang, made from the leaves, remains legal, and some states license bhang shops. It is offered to Shiva and drunk at Holi and Maha Shivaratri.
- **Iboga.** The Bwiti tradition of Gabon uses the root of *Tabernanthe iboga* in initiation.
- *Claude's gloss:* each of these traditions holds the substance inside a structure of meaning, ceremony and community. Keltner's account of awe, and the clinical finding that psychedelic therapy works through guided experience, describe that structure in scientific terms.
**links:** dmt · iboga · cannabis · keltner (awe) · psilocybin · war-on-drugs.

